content.text:
### Result
{
  "atBreak": {
    "phase": "break",
    "wave": 0,
    "breakT": 6.5
  },
  "after": {
    "phase": "wave",
    "wave": 1,
    "queue": 8,
    "title": "UNPAID GUESTS",
    "state": "WAVE 2 / 6"
  }
}
### Ran Playwright code
```js
await page.evaluate('() => { const g=__deadBlock; g.spawnQueue=[]; g.zombies.filter(z=>!z.dying).forEach(z=>g.killZombie(z,false)); for(let i=0;i<120;i++) g.update(1/60); const atBreak={phase:g.phase, wave:g.waveIndex, breakT:+g.breakT.toFixed(1)}; for(let i=0;i<540;i++) g.update(1/60); return {atBreak, after:{phase:g.phase, wave:g.waveIndex, queue:g.spawnQueue.length, title:document.getElementById(\'wave-title\').textContent, state:document.getElementById(\'wave-state\').textContent}}; }');
```

The bot reached wave 5 (index 5) — actually wave 5 = final wave 6? `waveIndex 5` means last wave, and died there; 109 kills, all barriers breached. That suggests the game is challenging but beatable with movement and repairs; stationary gets to last wave. Great. But wait, `phase dead` at wave 5 means died on final wave, close. Good balance. Maybe too hard for a mobile casual game? But Dead Block is a survival game. Fine.

However, our simulation didn't do repairs/traps, so a real player can win. Good.

Potential issue: when all barriers are destroyed, player dies quickly; expected.

Need maybe ensure that barriers can be repaired when destroyed? In design, `useRepair` refuses destroyed barrier ("BARRICADE IS BREACHED"). In Dead Block, you can rebuild barricades? The description says "Board up. Set traps." Three repairable barricades. If a barricade is destroyed, can the player rebuild it? Currently once destroyed, it cannot be repaired, only completeWave resets. That might be frustrating. In Dead Block, barricades can be repaired/rebuilt, but perhaps destroyed = open. The player can still survive. The instruction says "REPAIR barricade" and "barriers can be breached". Should we allow rebuilding a destroyed barricade at a higher scrap cost? It would improve gameplay. Maybe implement: if destroyed, allow repair for 4 scrap to restore 40% and set `destroyed=false`, show planks. But current `barrier.destroyed` toggles planks invisible, and `hitBarrier` evolves. It would be better. The user wants playable; being able to rebuild is a useful mechanic. But design decision can be noted. The UI button says repair 2 scrap; destroyed cannot. Maybe intentional: once breached, it's open for the rest of the wave. But allowing rebuild adds depth.

Should we change? Let's think. In Dead Block, you barricade windows/doors that zombies break and can re-board. So yes, rebuild should be allowed. Let's implement:
```
useRepair:
  const barrier = nearestBarrier();
  if (!barrier) toast...
  if (barrier.destroyed) {
    if (scrap < 4) toast('NEED 4 SCRAP TO REBUILD');
    scrap -= 4;
    barrier.destroyed = false; barrier.hp = barrier.maxHp * .45; planks visible; group pos reset;
    effects...
  } else {
    if hp>=max...; if scrap<2...
    scrap -= 2; hp +=130...
  }
```
But nearestBarrier returns only if within 3.6 and includes destroyed (yes).
`ui.setRepairReady` currently ready = near.hp < near.maxHp && scrap >=2, so destroyed counts since hp=0 < max. It will be armed. Good.
`updateNearBarrier` similar.

When rebuilt, zombies currently targeting barrier (their lane) will see `barrierOpen` recomputed next update and target barrier; good. The beacon updates.

This improves gameplay. Also maybe make repair cost scale; 4 scrap for rebuild is fine.

Need adjust toasts and effects. When rebuilding, `barrier.planks.forEach(p=>p.visible=true)`. Also `barrier.group.position.y=0; rotation.z=0`.
Use `effects.repair`, audio.repair, score += 50, toast 'BARRICADE REBUILT'. Good.
If hp>=max and not destroyed -> solid.
If scrap < 4 for destroyed -> need 4 scrap.

But UI button text shows cost 2 always. We could dynamically update cost when near destroyed barrier, but maybe not needed. Could show "REPAIR ✦2" and rebuilding costs 4; inconsistent. Alternatively cost 3 to rebuild and show "NEED 4 SCRAP"? Better to always cost 2 but restore only 45% on rebuild, simpler. But then destroying and rebuilding is cheap. Scrap is plentiful (99 cap). Let's use same 2 scrap for rebuild but restore 35% (maxHp*.35). That keeps consistency with UI. When destroyed, rebuild 35% for 2 scrap. Seems okay and encourages repair. But then player can stand at a destroyed barrier and spam rebuild 2 scrap each; scrap limited. Fine.

However, repairing a destroyed barrier while zombies are right there means they immediately attack again. Balanced.

Let's implement rebuild cost 3 maybe; but UI shows 2. We could update the button cost dynamically in UI based on near.destroyed. That's extra complexity. Could add a `setRepairCost(n)` method and update in updateNearBarrier. Let's do it simply:
- HTML `<em><i class="scrap-mini">✦</i><span id="repair-cost">2</span></em>`? Current markup: `<em><i class="scrap-mini">✦</i>2</em>`. Add span id. Then ui.setRepairCost(n) sets text. In game.updateNearBarrier, determine cost = near && near.destroyed ? 4 : 2; UI updates. Also if no near, default 2. That is transparent.
- But maybe not worth. Let's implement cost 2 for all repairs, restore 35% if destroyed, 130 hp if damaged. Consistent UI. Good. The player can rebuild for 2 scrap (same as patch). That makes destroyed barriers recoverable at low HP, which is fair.

Add in useRepair:
```
if (barrier.destroyed) {
  if (this.scrap < 2)...
  this.scrap -=2;
  barrier.destroyed=false;
  barrier.hp=barrier.maxHp*.35;
  barrier.planks.forEach(p=>p.visible=true);
  barrier.group.position.y=0; barrier.group.rotation.z=0;
  barrier.hp = Math.max(barrier.hp, barrier.maxHp*.35);
  this.score += 50;
  effects.repair(...);
  audio.build? audio.repair();
  toast('BARRICADE REBUILT','good');
  setScrap, setBarriers;
  return;
}
```
`barrier.beaconMat` color updates next world update. Good.
`barrier.shake=.35`; maybe.

Does `nearestBarrier` include destroyed? yes d distance.

This is a nice improvement. Let's add.

Also potential bug: `updateNearBarrier` sets repairReady only if hp<maxHp and scrap>=2; destroyed qualifies. good.

Now another gameplay issue: The UI toast "REPAIR THE LINE" during break tells player to repair. good.

Let's implement rebuild.

input.newString:
useRepair() {
  if (!['wave', 'break'].includes(this.phase)) return;
  const barrier = this.nearestBarrier();
  if (!barrier) { this.ui.toast('MOVE TO A BARRICADE', 'bad'); return; }
  if (this.scrap < 2) { this.ui.toast('NEED 2 SCRAP', 'bad'); return; }
  if (barrier.destroyed) {
    this.scrap -= 2;
    barrier.destroyed = false;
    barrier.hp = barrier.maxHp * .35;
    barrier.shake = .4;
    barrier.group.position.y = 0; barrier.group.rotation.z = 0;
    barrier.planks.forEach(p => p.visible = true);
    this.score += 60;
    const pos = barrier.pos.clone().add(new THREE.Vector3(0, 1.1, 0));
    this.effects.repair(pos);
    this.audio.repair();
    this.ui.toast('BARRICADE REBUILT', 'good');
    this.ui.setScrap(this.scrap);
    this.ui.setBarriers(this.world.barriers);
    return;
  }
  if (barrier.hp >= barrier.maxHp) { this.ui.toast('BARRICADE IS SOLID', 'bad'); return; }
  this.scrap -= 2;
  barrier.hp = Math.min(barrier.maxHp, barrier.hp + 130);
  barrier.shake = .35;
  this.score += 35;
  const pos = barrier.pos.clone().add(new THREE.Vector3(0, 1.4, 0));
  this.effects.repair(pos);
  this.audio.repair();
  this.ui.toast('BARRICADE PATCHED', 'good');
  this.ui.setScrap(this.scrap);
  this.ui.setBarriers(this.world.barriers);
}

